<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        ACCESS_MEDIA_LOCATION is required so that Android's media framework delivers an
        unredacted byte stream when the picked video is materialised to the local cache via
        ContentResolver.openInputStream. Without it the media module silently strips GPS
        location tags from the stream even when ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT is used. This permission
        reads — it does not collect — location that is already embedded in a specific file
        the user explicitly selects. The app requests no broad media access and never uses
        location for tracking or analytics. See CLAUDE.md for the full rationale.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_MEDIA_LOCATION" />

    <!--
        Launcher identity. The mark is the Tagalong hashtag-and-film-reel; the label is the
        functional name. See the `launcher-identity` spec — both are the app's identity on surfaces
        the app itself never draws.

        android:icon is load-bearing and easy to lose. Without it every resource under
        res/mipmap-*/ is dead weight and the platform silently renders its default app icon: the
        full Image Asset export shipped committed, referenced by nothing, green. The failure is
        quiet — no build error, no crash, no lint error — so LauncherIdentityTest guards it via
        ApplicationInfo.icon, which is 0 while this attribute is absent.

        There is deliberately NO <monochrome> layer in either adaptive-icon XML, and re-running
        Android Studio's Image Asset will put one back: as long as its Monochrome page is
        populated, Image Asset re-emits the element pointing at the colour foreground. That is the
        regression this comment exists for. The monochrome slot is a contract that its artwork is
        single-colour on transparent, because themed-icon launchers take that layer's alpha and
        flood it with the system accent colour. This mark is five-colour artwork with heavy dark
        outlines, so declaring it would claim a themed-icon capability the artwork does not
        support — the film reel disappears into the hashtag silhouette as one flat accent blob.
        An undeclared layer is an unclaimed capability; a fake one is a false claim. With no
        monochrome layer, themed launchers apply their own derived treatment to this icon.
        If AGP's MonochromeLauncherIcon warning bothers you, suppress it; do not restore the
        element. See openspec/changes/add-launcher-icon/design.md, Decision 1.

        android:roundIcon cannot be asserted at runtime — ApplicationInfo exposes no public
        roundIcon field — and `aapt2 dump badging` does not report it either. It is verified with
        `aapt2 dump xmltree` over AndroidManifest.xml, which shows both attributes resolved.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Tagalong">

        <!--
            launchMode="singleTask": the share intake contract is "consume exactly once".
            standard would stack a second MainActivity per share and leave stale launch
            intents behind; singleTop only dedupes when the instance is already on top.
            singleTask gives one instance and routes every arrival (launcher relaunch,
            re-share) through onNewIntent, where getIntent() is refreshed so a consumed
            share can never be re-parsed on relaunch.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
                Share target: the user picks a video in a gallery/file app and shares it
                to Tagalong directly. NO android:scheme here, deliberately: the canonical
                ACTION_SEND carries its file in EXTRA_STREAM with the intent's data field
                empty, and share sheets resolve with a type-only query — a scheme-bearing
                filter never matches that shape (verified on-device: `cmd package
                query-activities -a SEND -t video/mp4` omits this app, -d with a content
                uri includes it). The file:// / String / non-video bouncers live in
                MainActivity.parseSharedVideo instead, where an unusable payload degrades
                to the silent Home fallback. ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE is intentionally not
                declared: N videos is an unscoped UX (see add-share-target design D1).
                See AGENTS.md "Why the app also accepts ACTION_SEND" for the Play-review
                rationale.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="video/*" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

    </application>

</manifest>
